{"name":"Gliding Feather","hex":"#d7d8d9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gliding","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The action or motion of something that glides.","The hobby, sport, or act of flying a glider."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of glide"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gliding"},{"term":"feather","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A branching, hair-like structure that grows on the bodies of birds, used for flight, swimming, protection and display.","Long hair on the lower legs of a dog or horse, especially a draft horse, notably the Clydesdale breed. Narrowly only the rear hair."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or furnish with feathers;  to fletch.","To adorn, as if with feathers; to fringe."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/feather"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gliding","blocks":[{"text":"Gliding is a recreational activity and competitive air sport in which pilots fly unpowered aircraft known as gliders or sailplanes using naturally occurring currents of rising air in the atmosphere to remain airborne. The word soaring is also used for the sport."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Glider_finishing.jpg/330px-Glider_finishing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gliding","wikidata":"Q624161"},{"title":"Feather","blocks":[{"text":"Feathers are epidermal growths that form a distinctive outer covering, or plumage, on both avian (bird) and some non-avian dinosaurs and other archosaurs. They are the most complex integumentary structures found in vertebrates and an example of a complex evolutionary novelty. They are among the characteristics that distinguish the extant birds from other living groups."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Types_de_plumes._-_Larousse_pour_tous%2C_-1907-1910-.jpg/330px-Types_